@node	2.3, 2.4, 2.2, Chapter 2
@section Symbolic Data

All the compound data objects we have used so far were constructed ultimately
from numbers.  In this section we extend the representational capability of our
language by introducing the ability to work with arbitrary symbols as data.

@menu
* 2.3.1::            Quotation
* 2.3.2::            Example: Symbolic Differentiation
* 2.3.3::            Example: Representing Sets
* 2.3.4::            Example: Huffman Encoding Trees
@end menu

@node	2.3.1, 2.3.2, 2.3, 2.3
@subsection Quotation

If we can form compound data using symbols, we can have lists such as

@example rust
List::from_iter([sym("a"), sym("b"), sym("c"), sym("d")]);
List::from_iter([23_i128, 45, 17]);
List::from_iter([
    (sym("Norah"), 12),
    (sym("Molly"), 9),
    (sym("Anna"), 7),
    (sym("Lauren"), 6),
    (sym("Charlotte"), 4),
]);
@end example

@noindent
Where the book nests a two-element list for each name/age pair, this edition
reaches for a tuple: @code{List<T>} and @code{Nest<T>} are homogeneous, so
pairing a symbol with a number inside one of them needs a sum type, and the
section does not introduce one until @code{Expr} in @ref{2.3.2}.  A
@code{(Symbol, i128)} tuple is the honest translation of an ad hoc record
until then.

@noindent
Lists containing symbols can look just like the expressions of our
language:

@example rust
Expr::Product(
    Box::new(Expr::Sum(Box::new(Expr::Num(23)), Box::new(Expr::Num(45)))),
    Box::new(Expr::Sum(Box::new(Expr::Var(sym("x"))), Box::new(Expr::Num(9)))),
);
@end example

@noindent
Except that here they cannot: @code{Expr::Product(...)} is a value being
constructed by an ordinary function call, and no sequence of tokens that
builds it could also be read by @code{rustc} as executable code computing
something else.  Lisp lists and Lisp expressions share one surface syntax, so
the interpreter has to be told, token by token, which meaning is intended.
Rust's syntax for building a value and its syntax for computing one are
different from the start, which is exactly why the language we build in this
section never needs a @newterm{quote} operator to tell them apart.

@noindent
In Scheme, quoting is what makes @code{(a b)} available as a list of symbols
rather than a call to a function named @code{a} on a value named @code{b}.
@code{(list a b)} builds a list of the @newterm{values} of @code{a} and
@code{b}, not the symbols themselves.  This issue is well known in the
context of natural languages, where words and sentences may be regarded either
as semantic entities or as character strings (syntactic entities).  The common
practice in natural languages is to use quotation marks to indicate that a word
or a sentence is to be treated literally as a string of characters.  For
instance, the first letter of ``John'' is clearly ``J.''  If we tell somebody
``say your name aloud,'' we expect to hear that person's name.  However, if we
tell somebody ``say `your name' aloud,'' we expect to hear the words ``your
name.''  Note that we are forced to nest quotation marks to describe what
somebody else might say.@footnote{Allowing quotation in a language wreaks havoc
with the ability to reason about the language in simple terms, because it
destroys the notion that equals can be substituted for equals.  For example,
three is one plus two, but the word ``three'' is not the phrase ``one plus
two.''  Quotation is powerful because it gives us a way to build expressions
that manipulate other expressions (as we will see when we write an interpreter
in @ref{Chapter 4}). But allowing statements in a language that talk about
other statements in that language makes it very difficult to maintain any
coherent principle of what ``equals can be substituted for equals'' should
mean.  For example, if we know that the evening star is the morning star, then
from the statement ``the evening star is Venus'' we can deduce ``the morning
star is Venus.''  However, given that ``John knows that the evening star is
Venus'' we cannot infer that ``John knows that the morning star is Venus.''}

Rust never needs the marker that natural language, and Scheme, reach for here,
because a Rust value is never ambiguous between ``the thing itself'' and ``a
name that stands for the thing.''  @code{List::from_iter([sym("a"),
sym("b")])} is the list @code{(a b)}: there is no reading of this call under
which @code{sym("a")} could instead denote the value of some variable named
@code{a}, because @code{sym} always takes a string literal and always returns
a fresh @code{Symbol}.  The confusion the book's @code{'} solves does not
arise, so there is nothing here for a marker to disambiguate.@footnote{A Rust
string literal such as @code{"a"} is a further, unrelated distinction the book
does not need to draw at this point: @code{"a"} is a @code{&str}, a sequence
of characters meant to be printed, and @code{Symbol::from("a")} is the
one-character conversion that turns it into the interned, @code{==}-comparable
value this section manipulates as data.  The two are never confused, because
Rust's double quotes always mean ``string'' and nothing else.}

We can follow this same practice to identify lists and symbols that are to be
treated as data objects rather than as expressions to be evaluated.  Now we
can distinguish between symbols and their values:

@example rust
let a = 1;
let b = 2;

List::from_iter([a, b]);
// => (1 2)

List::from_iter([sym("a"), sym("b")]);
// => (a b)
@end example

@noindent
The first call builds a list of the numbers @code{a} and @code{b} are bound
to; the second builds a list of the two-character symbols @code{a} and
@code{b}, which share nothing with the variables above but their spelling.
Because @code{List<T>} is homogeneous, there is no third call mixing a
variable's value with a symbol of the same name in one list the way the
book's @code{(list 'a b)} does; the type checker would refuse to compile a
@code{List<i128>} literal containing a @code{Symbol}, which is the same
distinction the book is illustrating, enforced one level earlier.

@noindent
Symbols also let us read the parts of a list back out:

@example rust
let abc: List<Symbol> = List::from_iter([sym("a"), sym("b"), sym("c")]);
println!("@{@}", abc.car().cloned().expect("cons cell"));
// => a
println!("@{@}", abc.cdr().expect("cons cell"));
// => (b c)
@end example

@noindent
In keeping with this, the empty list is simply @code{List::Nil}: a variant of
the type itself, constructed the same way regardless of element type, never a
loose global variable that quoting would need to dispense with the way the
book's discussion of @code{nil} implies.@footnote{Strictly, this also settles a
question the book raises separately: whether a leading quotation mark is a
primitive notation of its own, or an abbreviation for a special form
@code{quote} that a reader expands before the interpreter ever sees it.  This
edition has no reader to do that expanding, so there is only one spelling to
begin with: @code{List::from_iter([sym("a"), sym("b"), sym("c")])} already is
the one way to build the list @code{(a b c)}, called directly rather than
abbreviated by a mark and later expanded into an equivalent call.  Exercise
2.55 asks what a reader would have built out of doubled quotation, without
ever needing to run one.}

One additional primitive used in manipulating symbols is @code{==} on
@code{Symbol}, which takes two symbols and tests whether they are the
same.@footnote{Because @code{Symbol} is @code{Rc<str>}, @code{==} compares
the underlying characters, so two symbols built from the same string always
compare equal, whether or not they happen to share one allocation.  Such a
definition skirts a deep issue that we are not yet ready to address: the
meaning of ``sameness'' in a programming language.  We will return to this in
@ref{Chapter 3} (@ref{3.1.3}).}
Using @code{==}, we can implement a useful procedure called @code{memq}.  This
takes two arguments, an item and a list.  If the item is not contained in
the list (i.e., is not @code{==} to any item in the list), then @code{memq}
returns @code{None}.  Otherwise, it returns the sublist of the list beginning with
the first occurrence of the item:

@example rust
/// Finds `item` in `xs` by `eq?`-style comparison: the book's `memq`.
pub fn memq<T: PartialEq + Clone>(item: &T, xs: &List<T>) -> Option<List<T>> @{
    let mut cursor = xs;
    loop @{
        match cursor @{
            List::Nil => return None,
            List::Cons(x, rest) => @{
                if x == item @{
                    return Some(cursor.clone());
                @}
                cursor = rest;
            @}
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Because @code{memq} is generic over any @code{T: PartialEq + Clone}, it works
unchanged whether the list holds flat symbols or the nested @code{Nest<Symbol>}
trees @ref{2.2} built; the book's Scheme version gets this generality for
free from being untyped, while here the compiler proves it once at the
function's own definition.  For example, the value of

@example rust
memq(&sym("apple"), &List::from_iter([sym("pear"), sym("banana"), sym("prune")]))
    .map_or_else(|| "false".to_string(), |l| l.to_string());
@end example

@noindent
is @code{"false"}, whereas the value of

@example rust
let xs: List<Nest<Symbol>> = List::from_iter([
    leaf(sym("x")),
    sub(&[leaf(sym("apple")), leaf(sym("sauce"))]),
    leaf(sym("y")),
    leaf(sym("apple")),
    leaf(sym("pear")),
]);
memq(&Nest::Leaf(sym("apple")), &xs)
    .map_or_else(|| "false".to_string(), |l| l.to_string());
@end example

@noindent
is @code{"(apple pear)"}: the first @code{apple} is buried inside a
@code{Nest::Sub}, a different variant from the bare @code{Nest::Leaf(sym("apple"))}
being searched for, so it is skipped exactly as the book's untagged sublist
is skipped by @code{eq?}.

@quotation
@emph{SICP exercise 2.53 is about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it; the exercise below keeps the number and teaches the same section material.}

@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.53}Exercise 2.53:} What value, or what printed
form, does each of the following produce?  Answer from the types alone where
you can, without running anything.

@example rust
List::from_iter([sym("a"), sym("b"), sym("c")])
sub(&[sub(&[leaf(sym("george"))])])

let pairs: List<Nest<Symbol>> = List::from_iter([
    sub(&[leaf(sym("x1")), leaf(sym("x2"))]),
    sub(&[leaf(sym("y1")), leaf(sym("y2"))]),
]);
pairs.cdr()
pairs.cdr().and_then(List::car)

let short_list: List<Symbol> = List::from_iter([sym("a"), sym("short"), sym("list")]);
// Could short_list.car() ever be a value that is itself a compound list?
// Answer in terms of List<Symbol>'s type, not by tracing the call.

let outer = sub(&[
    sub(&[leaf(sym("red")), leaf(sym("shoes"))]),
    sub(&[leaf(sym("blue")), leaf(sym("socks"))]),
]);
// as a List<Nest<Symbol>>:
memq(&Nest::Leaf(sym("red")), &outer_as_list)

let flat: List<Symbol> = List::from_iter([sym("red"), sym("shoes"), sym("blue"), sym("socks")]);
memq(&sym("red"), &flat)
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.54}Exercise 2.54:} Two @code{Nest<Symbol>} values are said to be
equal, call the comparison @code{my_equal}, if they contain equal elements arranged in the same order.  For
example,

@example rust
my_equal(
    &sub(&[leaf(sym("this")), leaf(sym("is")), leaf(sym("a")), leaf(sym("list"))]),
    &sub(&[leaf(sym("this")), leaf(sym("is")), leaf(sym("a")), leaf(sym("list"))]),
);
@end example

@noindent
is @code{true}, but

@example rust
my_equal(
    &sub(&[leaf(sym("this")), leaf(sym("is")), leaf(sym("a")), leaf(sym("list"))]),
    &sub(&[leaf(sym("this")), sub(&[leaf(sym("is")), leaf(sym("a"))]), leaf(sym("list"))]),
);
@end example

@noindent
is @code{false}.  To be more precise, we can define @code{my_equal}
recursively in terms of the basic @code{==} equality of symbols by saying that
@code{a} and @code{b} are equal if they are both leaves and the symbols are
@code{==}, or if they are both branches such that every corresponding pair of
their items is recursively equal, in order.  Implement this yourself, as a
recursive function: @code{Nest<Symbol>} already derives a structural
@code{PartialEq}, but the point of the exercise is writing the recursion that
derive gives you for free, not calling it.@footnote{In practice,
programmers use @code{my_equal} to compare lists that contain numbers as well as
symbols.  Numbers are not considered to be symbols.  The question of whether
two numerically equal numbers (as tested by @code{==}) are also the same
allocation is highly implementation-dependent.  A better definition of
@code{my_equal} would also stipulate that if @code{a} and @code{b} are both
numbers, then @code{a} and @code{b} are equal if they are numerically equal.}
@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{SICP exercise 2.55 is about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it; the exercise below keeps the number and teaches the same section material.}

@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.55}Exercise 2.55:} This edition has no reader, so
``@code{''abracadabra}'' is not something a program ever types at a prompt.
But we can still ask what nested quotation @emph{would} build, by
representing exactly what a reader constructs, as data, and nothing more:

@example rust
enum Quoted @{
    Sym(Symbol),
    Pair(Box<Quoted>, Box<Quoted>),
    Nil,
@}
@end example

@noindent
A single quote @code{'x} is reader sugar for the two-element list
@code{(quote x)}; a doubled quote @code{''abracadabra} is sugar for
@code{(quote (quote abracadabra))}.  Build the value that @code{''abracadabra}
would read as, using only @code{Quoted::Sym}, @code{Quoted::Pair}, and
@code{Quoted::Nil} (an interior helper such as @code{list2(a, b)} for the
two-element case is fair game).  Write a @code{Display} impl that prints a
@code{Quoted} list the way the book prints Scheme lists, then predict what
your @code{Display} impl prints for the value you built, and what its
@code{car} is.  Explain your answer without appealing to a reader at all: say
directly, in terms of the @code{Quoted} value's shape, why the outer
@code{Pair}'s first element is the symbol @code{quote} rather than
@code{abracadabra}.
@end quotation

@node	2.3.2, 2.3.3, 2.3.1, 2.3
@subsection Example: Symbolic Differentiation

As an illustration of symbol manipulation and a further illustration of data
abstraction, consider the design of a procedure that performs symbolic
differentiation of algebraic expressions.  We would like the procedure to take
as arguments an algebraic expression and a variable and to return the
derivative of the expression with respect to the variable.  For example, if the
arguments to the procedure are @math{{ax^2 + bx + c}} and @math{x}, the
procedure should return @math{{2ax + b}}.  Symbolic differentiation is of
special historical significance in Lisp.  It was one of the motivating examples
behind the development of a computer language for symbol manipulation.
Furthermore, it marked the beginning of the line of research that led to the
development of powerful systems for symbolic mathematical work, which are
currently being used by a growing number of applied mathematicians and
physicists.

In developing the symbolic-differentiation program, we will follow the same
strategy of data abstraction that we followed in developing the rational-number
system of @ref{2.1.1}.  That is, we will first define a differentiation
algorithm that operates on abstract objects such as ``sums,'' ``products,'' and
``variables'' without worrying about how these are to be represented.  Only
afterward will we address the representation problem.  In Scheme, that
separation is total, because a list-based representation can stay hidden
behind procedures indefinitely.  In Rust, it is smaller: naming the abstract
objects at all means giving them a type, and a closed @code{enum} is both the
abstract interface @code{deriv} is written against and its concrete
representation at once, since there is no other representation this edition
will ever install behind it.

@subsubheading The differentiation program with abstract data

To keep things simple, we will consider a very simple
symbolic-differentiation program that handles expressions that are built up
using only the operations of addition and multiplication with two arguments.
Differentiation of any such expression can be carried out by applying the
following reduction rules:
@displaymath
{{dc \over dx} \,=\, 0,} 
  \quad {\hbox{for } c \hbox{ a constant }} {\hbox{or a variable }} {\hbox{different from } x,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
dc
-- = 0  for c a constant, or a variable different from x
dx

dx
-- = 1
dx

d(u + v)   du   dv
-------- = -- + --
   dx      dx   dx

d(uv)     / dv \     / du \
----- = u | -- | + v | -- |
 dx       \ dx /     \ dx /
@end example

@end ifinfo
@displaymath
\eqalign{
{dx \over dx}         &=   1, \cr
{d(u + v) \over dx}   &=   {du \over dx} + {dv \over dx}, \cr
{d(uv) \over dx}      &=   u \kern0.1em {dv \over dx} + v \kern0.1em {du \over dx}.
}
@end displaymath
@noindent
Observe that the latter two rules are recursive in nature.  That is, to obtain
the derivative of a sum we first find the derivatives of the terms and add
them.  Each of the terms may in turn be an expression that needs to be
decomposed.  Decomposing into smaller and smaller pieces will eventually
produce pieces that are either constants or variables, whose derivatives will
be either 0 or 1.

To embody these rules in a procedure we indulge in a little wishful thinking,
as we did in designing the rational-number implementation.  If we had a means
for representing algebraic expressions, we should be able to tell whether an
expression is a sum, a product, a constant, or a variable.  We should be able
to extract the parts of an expression.  For a sum, for example we want to be
able to extract the addend (first term) and the augend (second term).  We
should also be able to construct expressions from parts.  The type that grants
all of this at once is:

@example rust
pub enum Expr @{
    /// A number.
    Num(i128),
    /// A variable, identified by its symbol.
    Var(Symbol),
    /// A sum of two expressions.
    Sum(Box<Expr>, Box<Expr>),
    /// A product of two expressions.
    Product(Box<Expr>, Box<Expr>),
@}
@end example

@noindent
The book's ten assumed procedures collapse into this one type: @code{sum?}
and @code{product?} become the @code{Sum}/@code{Product} match arms below,
@code{addend}/@code{augend} and @code{multiplier}/@code{multiplicand} become
the two boxed fields those arms bind, and @code{make-sum}/@code{make-product}
become the two closures @code{deriv_via} takes as parameters, so that changing
how sums and products are built (the section's whole point, reached again
below) never touches @code{deriv_via} itself.  @code{variable?} is absorbed
into the @code{Var} match arm, and @code{same-variable?} survives as
@code{is_same_variable} because @code{Var}'s field is a value that still
needs comparing, not merely a shape to match:

@example rust
/// Checks whether an expression is equal to a given number: the book's
/// `=number?`.
pub fn is_number(exp: &Expr, num: i128) -> bool @{
    matches!(exp, Expr::Num(x) if *x == num)
@}

/// Checks whether `exp` is the variable `var`: the book's
/// `(same-variable? exp var)`.
pub fn is_same_variable(exp: &Expr, var: &Symbol) -> bool @{
    matches!(exp, Expr::Var(x) if x == var)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Using these, and pattern matching in place of the primitive predicate
@code{number?}, we can express the differentiation rules as the following
function, which takes the sum and product constructors as parameters so that
the book's later promise, ``we won't change @code{deriv} at all,'' is literal:
this function is written once and never edited again, no matter which pair of
constructors it is handed.

@example rust
pub fn deriv_via(
    exp: &Expr,
    var: &Symbol,
    make_sum: &impl Fn(Expr, Expr) -> Result<Expr, SchemeError>,
    make_product: &impl Fn(Expr, Expr) -> Result<Expr, SchemeError>,
) -> Result<Expr, SchemeError> @{
    match exp @{
        Expr::Num(_) => Ok(Expr::Num(0)),
        Expr::Var(x) => Ok(Expr::Num(i128::from(x == var))),
        Expr::Sum(a1, a2) => make_sum(
            deriv_via(a1, var, make_sum, make_product)?,
            deriv_via(a2, var, make_sum, make_product)?,
        ),
        Expr::Product(m1, m2) => @{
            let left = make_product((**m1).clone(), deriv_via(m2, var, make_sum, make_product)?)?;
            let right = make_product(deriv_via(m1, var, make_sum, make_product)?, (**m2).clone())?;
            make_sum(left, right)
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
This function incorporates the complete differentiation algorithm.  Since the
@code{Expr::Num}/@code{Expr::Var}/@code{Expr::Sum}/@code{Expr::Product} match
is exhaustive, the compiler proves every case is handled; the book's final
@code{(else (error "unknown expression type: DERIV" exp))} clause has no
counterpart here, because there is no fifth variant left for it to catch.

@subsubheading Representing algebraic expressions

Two variables are the same if the symbols representing them are @code{==},
which is exactly @code{is_same_variable} above.  Printing an expression back
in the book's parenthesized prefix notation is a @code{Display} impl over the
same four variants:

@example rust
impl fmt::Display for Expr @{
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result @{
        match self @{
            Expr::Num(n) => write!(f, "@{n@}"),
            Expr::Var(x) => write!(f, "@{x@}"),
            Expr::Sum(a, b) => write!(f, "(+ @{a@} @{b@})"),
            Expr::Product(a, b) => write!(f, "(* @{a@} @{b@})"),
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Now all that is missing is the pair of constructors @code{deriv_via} expects.
The naive versions build a sum or a product without simplifying it at all:

@example rust
pub fn make_sum_unsimplified(a1: Expr, a2: Expr) -> Expr @{
    Expr::Sum(Box::new(a1), Box::new(a2))
@}
pub fn make_product_unsimplified(m1: Expr, m2: Expr) -> Expr @{
    Expr::Product(Box::new(m1), Box::new(m2))
@}

pub fn deriv_unsimplified(exp: &Expr, var: &Symbol) -> Result<Expr, SchemeError> @{
    deriv_via(
        exp,
        var,
        &|a, b| Ok(make_sum_unsimplified(a, b)),
        &|a, b| Ok(make_product_unsimplified(a, b)),
    )
@}
@end example

@noindent
Thus, we need only combine these with the algorithm as embodied by
@code{deriv_via} to have a working symbolic-differentiation program.
Let us look at some examples of its behavior:

@example rust
let sum = Expr::Sum(Box::new(Expr::Var(sym("x"))), Box::new(Expr::Num(3)));
deriv_unsimplified(&sum, &sym("x"))?;
// => (+ 1 0)

let product = Expr::Product(Box::new(Expr::Var(sym("x"))), Box::new(Expr::Var(sym("y"))));
deriv_unsimplified(&product, &sym("x"))?;
// => (+ (* x 0) (* 1 y))

let nested = Expr::Product(
    Box::new(Expr::Product(Box::new(Expr::Var(sym("x"))), Box::new(Expr::Var(sym("y"))))),
    Box::new(Expr::Sum(Box::new(Expr::Var(sym("x"))), Box::new(Expr::Num(3)))),
);
deriv_unsimplified(&nested, &sym("x"))?;
// => (+ (* (* x y) (+ 1 0)) (* (+ (* x 0) (* 1 y)) (+ x 3)))
@end example

@noindent
The program produces answers that are correct; however, they are unsimplified.
It is true that
@displaymath
{d(xy) \over dx} \,=\, {x \cdot 0} + {1 \cdot y,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
d(xy)
----- = x * 0 + 1 * y
 dx
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
but we would like the program to know that @math{{x \cdot 0 = 0}}, @math{{1 \cdot y = y}},
and @math{{0 + y = y}}.  The answer for the second example should have been
simply @code{y}.  As the third example shows, this becomes a serious issue when
the expressions are complex.

Our difficulty is much like the one we encountered with the rational-number
implementation: we haven't reduced answers to simplest form.  To accomplish the
rational-number reduction, we needed to change only the constructors and the
selectors of the implementation.  We can adopt a similar strategy here.  We
won't change @code{deriv_via} at all.  Instead, we will change @code{make_sum} so
that if both summands are numbers, @code{make_sum} will add them and return
their sum.  Also, if one of the summands is 0, then @code{make_sum} will return
the other summand:

@example rust
/// # Errors
/// [`SchemeError::Overflow`] when both summands are numbers whose sum
/// leaves the `i128` range.
pub fn make_sum(a1: Expr, a2: Expr) -> Result<Expr, SchemeError> @{
    if is_number(&a1, 0) @{
        return Ok(a2);
    @}
    if is_number(&a2, 0) @{
        return Ok(a1);
    @}
    if let (Expr::Num(x), Expr::Num(y)) = (&a1, &a2) @{
        return Ok(Expr::Num(x.checked_add(*y).ok_or(SchemeError::Overflow)?));
    @}
    Ok(Expr::Sum(Box::new(a1), Box::new(a2)))
@}
@end example

@noindent
This uses @code{is_number} above, which checks whether an expression is
equal to a given number.  Similarly, we will change @code{make_product} to
build in the rules that 0 times anything is 0 and 1 times anything is the
thing itself:

@example rust
/// # Errors
/// [`SchemeError::Overflow`] when both factors are numbers whose
/// product leaves the `i128` range.
pub fn make_product(m1: Expr, m2: Expr) -> Result<Expr, SchemeError> @{
    if is_number(&m1, 0) || is_number(&m2, 0) @{
        return Ok(Expr::Num(0));
    @}
    if is_number(&m1, 1) @{
        return Ok(m2);
    @}
    if is_number(&m2, 1) @{
        return Ok(m1);
    @}
    if let (Expr::Num(x), Expr::Num(y)) = (&m1, &m2) @{
        return Ok(Expr::Num(x.checked_mul(*y).ok_or(SchemeError::Overflow)?));
    @}
    Ok(Expr::Product(Box::new(m1), Box::new(m2)))
@}

pub fn deriv(exp: &Expr, var: &Symbol) -> Result<Expr, SchemeError> @{
    deriv_via(exp, var, &make_sum, &make_product)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Both refined constructors fold their numeric case with @code{checked_add}
and @code{checked_mul}: this edition's numbers are fixed-width @code{i128},
not Scheme's unbounded integers, so a differentiation that overflows returns
@code{SchemeError::Overflow} instead of silently wrapping.  Here is how this
version works on our three examples:

@example rust
deriv(&sum, &sym("x"))?;
// => 1

deriv(&product, &sym("x"))?;
// => y

deriv(&nested, &sym("x"))?;
// => (+ (* x y) (* y (+ x 3)))
@end example

@noindent
Although this is quite an improvement, the third example shows that there is
still a long way to go before we get a program that puts expressions into a
form that we might agree is ``simplest.''  The problem of algebraic
simplification is complex because, among other reasons, a form that may be
simplest for one purpose may not be for another.

