@subsubheading Sets as ordered lists

One way to speed up our set operations is to change the representation so that
the set elements are listed in increasing order.  Because this edition's sets
are already the numeric type @code{i128}, we can compare elements directly
with @code{<} and @code{>} without needing to agree on a way to order
non-numeric objects first.  Whereas our first
representation above allowed us to represent the set @math{{\{1, 3, 6, 10\}}} by listing
the elements in any order, our new representation allows only @code{[1, 3, 6, 10]}.

One advantage of ordering shows up in @code{element_of_set_ordered}: in checking for
the presence of an item, we no longer have to scan the entire set.  If we reach
a set element that is larger than the item we are looking for, then we know
that the item is not in the set:

@example rust
pub fn element_of_set_ordered(x: i128, set: &[i128]) -> bool @{
    match set.first() @{
        None => false,
        Some(&y) => match x.cmp(&y) @{
            Ordering::Equal => true,
            Ordering::Less => false,
            Ordering::Greater => element_of_set_ordered(x, &set[1..]),
        @},
    @}
@}
@end example

How many steps does this save?  In the worst case, the item we are looking for
may be the largest one in the set, so the number of steps is the same as for
the unordered representation.  On the other hand, if we search for items of
many different sizes we can expect that sometimes we will be able to stop
searching at a point near the beginning of the slice and that other times we
will still need to examine most of the slice.  On the average we should expect
to have to examine about half of the items in the set.  Thus, the average
number of steps required will be about @math{{n / 2}}.  This is still
@math{{\Theta(n)}} growth, but it does save us, on the average, a factor of 2
in number of steps over the previous implementation.

We obtain a more impressive speedup with @code{intersection_set_ordered}.  In the
unordered representation this operation required @math{{\Theta(n^2)}} steps,
because we performed a complete scan of @code{set2} for each element of
@code{set1}.  But with the ordered representation, we can use a more clever
method: walk both slices in step with two indices, taking the smaller head (or
the shared head once, advancing both) at each step.  The book writes this
recursively, which shares list structure for free in Scheme; this edition
writes the walk as a loop instead, so the @code{Vec} result is still built
just once rather than being copied at every recursive return, keeping the
@math{{\Theta(n)}} cost genuine and not an artifact of a persistent list this
host type does not have:

@example rust
pub fn intersection_set_ordered(set1: &[i128], set2: &[i128]) -> Vec<i128> @{
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = 0;
    while i < set1.len() && j < set2.len() @{
        match set1[i].cmp(&set2[j]) @{
            Ordering::Equal => @{
                out.push(set1[i]);
                i += 1;
                j += 1;
            @}
            Ordering::Less => i += 1,
            Ordering::Greater => j += 1,
        @}
    @}
    out
@}
@end example

@noindent
To estimate the number of steps required by this process, observe that at each
step we reduce the intersection problem to computing intersections of smaller
sets---removing the first element from @code{set1} or @code{set2} or both.
Thus, the number of steps required is at most the sum of the sizes of
@code{set1} and @code{set2}, rather than the product of the sizes as with the
unordered representation.  This is @math{{\Theta(n)}} growth rather than
@math{{\Theta(n^2)}}---a considerable speedup, even for sets of moderate size.

